            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Mother Teresa</strong><br>\nAgnes Gonxha Bojaxhiu, known as Mother Teresa, was born on August 26, 1910, in Skopje, Macedonia. From a young age, she was fascinated by stories of missionaries serving in Bengal, which sparked her interest in religious life. At the age of 18, she left home to join the Sisters of Loreto in Ireland, never to see her mother or sister again. She was sent to India, where she took her initial vows as a nun in 1931.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Becoming Mother Teresa</strong><br>\nAfter taking her vows, Agnes chose the name Teresa to honor Saints Therese of Lisieux and Teresa of Avila. She taught at St. Mary's High School in Kolkata, where she became deeply concerned with the poverty around her. In 1946, she felt \"the call within a call\" to serve the poorest of the poor while retaining her life as a nun.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Missionaries of Charity</strong><br>\nIn 1950, Mother Teresa founded the Missionaries of Charity, a Roman Catholic religious congregation dedicated to caring for those in great need. She started with a small school in Kolkata but soon extended the congregation's service to the wider community. The order provides free services for people considered the poorest and the sickest.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
