          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Expanding the Work Globally</strong><br>\nMother Teresa's work rapidly expanded beyond India, and by the 1960s, she had established a leprosy outreach service. By 1979, the Missionaries of Charity operated 30 homes around the world. Mother Teresa became a global symbol of charitable work, and her order continued to grow under her leadership, extending to countries around the world.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Recognition and Criticism</strong><br>\nMother Teresa's humanitarian work earned her international recognition, including the Nobel Peace Prize in 1979. However, she also faced criticism. Some accused her of proselytizing to the vulnerable and criticized the conditions of the homes she ran. Despite controversies, her commitment to her work remained unwavering.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Her Philosophy and Teachings</strong><br>\nMother Teresa believed in doing small things with great love. She emphasized the dignity of all human life and encouraged her followers to serve others as a way of expressing their love for God. Her teachings inspired thousands, leading many to volunteer and donate to causes that supported the poor.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
