          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Mother Teresa's Later Years</strong><br>\nIn her later years, Mother Teresa's health began to decline. She suffered from heart, lung, and kidney problems but continued her work almost until her death. She stepped down as head of the Missionaries of Charity in 1997, leaving a legacy of compassion and service.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Path to Sainthood</strong><br>\nAfter her death on September 5, 1997, Mother Teresa was beatified by Pope John Paul II, and in 2016, she was canonized as Saint Teresa of Calcutta. Her canonization was marked by celebrations in India and across the globe, as well as debates about her life's work.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Continuing Influence</strong><br>\nMother Teresa's legacy continues through the work of the Missionaries of Charity, which has over 4,500 sisters and is active in 133 countries. The organization continues to aid the poor, sick, orphaned, and dying while maintaining the spirit of its founder's devotion to charity and humility.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
